{
 "id": "2024-zhu-shopping",
 "name": "Shopping Mall Facade Color Emotion, Aesthetics and Entry",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: ten computer renderings of the same modern shopping mall street scene (glass and solid curtain walls, with sculpture, road, greenery, pedestrians and traffic), differing only in the color of the solid facade elements: warm red (5R) and yellow (5Y), cool blue (5B) and green (5G) and neutral (N), each in a high and a low value, chosen by five senior architects from the CBCC Chinese Architectural Color Card. Participants first answer the paper's background questions (gender, age group, marital status, education, monthly income), read the paper's opening instruction (imagine walking in the busy commercial center of an unfamiliar city looking for a shopping mall), and then see the ten scenes one after another, each followed by a black screen for 10 s as in the paper. For each scene they answer the paper's Appendix A questionnaire: current emotional state on six 5-box bipolar pairs (pleasure: happy-unhappy, satisfied-unsatisfied, contented-melancholic; arousal: stimulated-relaxed, excited-calm, awake-sleepy), how the mall's colors make them feel on six aesthetic items from strongly agree to strongly oppose (classical: elegance, comfort, handicraft; expressive: sophistication, creativity, innovativeness), and Will you choose to enter this shopping mall? (yes / no). Rebuilt from Zhu, Liu & Chen (2024), The Influence of Emotional Response and Aesthetic Perception of Shopping Mall Facade Color on Entry Decisions—Evidence from the Yangtze River Delta Region of China, Buildings 14(8): 2302, with the renderings cropped from its Figure 2 and the questionnaire, colors and background categories from its Appendix A and Tables 1-2 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: S01-S10 = scenarios 1-10 of Table 1; answers are in emotion (1 = box next to the left adjective as printed, e.g. happy, 5 = next to the right one, e.g. unhappy), aesthetic (5 = strongly agree, 1 = strongly oppose) and enter, keyed by scene id; hue, tone and Munsell value of each scene are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: reliability and validity of the four dimensions (pleasure, arousal, classical and expressive aesthetic perception), Pearson correlations, ANOVA of the dimensions by hue (warm, cool, neutral) and t-tests by value (high, low), and logistic regression of the entry decision on hue, value, emotions and aesthetic perceptions; the paper (149 working adults aged 25-60, all passing an Ishihara test, in a conference room in front of a 70-inch screen) found neutral colors gave the highest pleasure and aesthetic perception, warm tones and high value more positive emotions and aesthetic perceptions than cool and dark ones, and pleasure the key predictor of entering. Differences from the original study: (1) online on the participant's own screen instead of a 70-inch 4K television in a room lit at 500-800 lx, with a paper questionnaire, and no Ishihara color vision test (a self-report question is added instead); (2) the scenes are shown in the paper's scenario order (1-10), which the paper does not state; (3) the background question wording and the color vision question are ours; the categories follow Table 2, with an under-25 age group, a high-school-or-below level, an above-30,000 income group and other or prefer-not-to-say options added, since the paper recruited only employees of one Nanjing commercial management company and anyone can take part here; (4) the renderings are the figure copies (about 1300 px wide), not the 3840 x 2160 px originals.",
